  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{3n+2}}{{x}^{2n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ Find the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<x$\n\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{3n+2}}{{x}^{2n}} \\\\  & ={x}^{\\,3n+2-2n} \\\\  & ={x}^{\\,n+2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=x.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 1.\n$$",
